# Student LMS Coding Guidelines

## Core Standards
- Use TypeScript strict mode patterns; avoid `any` unless absolutely necessary.
- Reuse existing components, utilities.
- Prefer `shadcn/ui` and Radix UI primitives wherever possible before building custom UI from scratch.
- Prefer icons from a maintained icon library (for example, `@phosphor-icons/react`) over image URLs or custom icon assets wherever possible.
- Keep components focused and readable; extract reusable logic when duplication appears.
- Keep file size minimal; max file length is 200 lines. Split into smaller components/hooks/utils when approaching this limit.
- Handle edge cases explicitly (empty states, invalid inputs, null/undefined values).

## API Architecture
- Expose all backend functionality as REST API routes only (file routes under
  `src/routes/api/**` with `server.handlers` for GET/POST/etc.). Do not add new
  TanStack `createServerFn` server functions for new work.
- Structure each endpoint as route handler → handler (`src/server/api/**/handlers`)
  → service (`src/server/api/**/*.service.ts`), and call it from the client via
  the typed `fetchJson` helpers in `src/lib/api/**`.
- Access the database through the Drizzle query builder (`db.select`, `db.insert`,
  `db.update`, `db.delete` from `@/db`). Avoid raw `sql` template queries unless a
  query genuinely cannot be expressed with the builder.

## Testing Requirements
- Add or update tests for every non-trivial behavior change.
- Prefer behavior-driven tests with Testing Library over implementation details.
- Cover positive path, fallback/edge case, and critical UI state transitions.
- Keep tests deterministic; avoid timing-sensitive assertions where possible.
- Write tests side by side with implementation in the same PR; do not defer tests to follow-up work.
- Use `vitest` for unit tests and keep test files colocated or clearly paired with source files (for example, `foo.ts` + `foo.test.ts`).
- Coverage target for this repo is 100%; at minimum, any touched module must stay at 100% statement/branch/function/line coverage.

## Testing Documentation
- Keep `docs/testing/feature-test-matrix.md` updated for every touched feature.
- Add or update `docs/testing/features/<feature>.md` when tests are added/changed/removed.
- Complete `docs/testing/pr-checklist.md` in every PR that changes feature behavior or test setup.
- Ensure documented test commands are valid and runnable.

## Styling and UX
- Follow existing Tailwind and component style conventions in the same feature area.
- Avoid hardcoded magic values when a project token or shared constant exists.
- Preserve accessibility basics (semantic elements, interactive affordances, readable text).

## Automation Test Hooks
- Add a stable `data-testid` to every meaningful UI element so end-to-end /
  automation suites can target it without depending on copy, styling, or DOM
  structure. Cover: section/container roots, interactive controls
  (buttons, tabs, links, inputs), repeated list items, and key text/status
  elements.
- Use `data-testid` for this — do not add `id`s or class names purely for
  testing, and never key styling off the test id.
- Naming: kebab-case, feature-prefixed — `data-testid="<feature>-<element>"`
  (e.g. `dashboard-welcome-banner`, `dashboard-schedule-card`). For repeated
  list items reuse one id and query all; when a stable domain id exists, suffix
  it (e.g. `dashboard-announcement-item-<id>`).
- Every new UI element (and any UI element you touch) must carry a
  `data-testid`. Do not rename/remove an existing one without updating its
  consumers.

## Analytics / Event Tracking (GA / GTM)
- Every new interactive element (CTA, button, tab, link, card, filter, menu
  item, etc.) must fire a GA/GTM event so nothing ships untracked. Adding a
  clickable without an event is incomplete work — treat it like a missing
  `data-testid`.
- Fire events by pushing to the GTM `dataLayer` via the shared helper
  `pushGtmEvent(event, params)` in `src/utils/gtm.ts`. Do not call
  `window.dataLayer.push` / `window.gtag` directly and do not add new
  ad-hoc helpers.
  - Dashboard surfaces: use `pushDashboardEvent(event, params)` from
    `src/components/features/dashboard/shared/dashboardAnalytics.ts`; every
    dashboard event name must contain the word `dashboard`
    (prefix `l_dashboard_`).
  - Learn surfaces: use `pushLearnEvent(event, params)` /
    `learnEntityEvent(type, action, id)` from
    `src/components/features/learn/shared/learnAnalytics.ts`
    (prefix `l_learn_`).
- Embed the relevant DB row id in the event name (`..._id_<id>`) whenever the
  click targets a specific entity, and use the entity type in the name (e.g.
  `l_learn_lecture_card_click_id_42`, `l_dashboard_fee_payment_cta_id_7`). If
  the id is not already in scope, plumb it through — do not drop it.
- Pass identifying context (ids, entity type, title, tab, page, filter key,
  source surface, etc.) as the `params` object so events are useful for
  segmentation. Fire the event synchronously at click time, before any
  navigation/await, and keep it additive — never change existing
  navigation/mutation behavior.
- Do not track noisy, high-frequency, non-intent signals (e.g. video
  scrubbing, per-keystroke input, volume drags); track the meaningful,
  debounced/committed action instead.

## PR Quality Bar
- Ensure `npm run test` and `npm run lint` pass for touched code.
- Keep changes scoped to the request; do not refactor unrelated areas.
- Add concise code comments only where logic is non-obvious.

## Seed Framework
- Each seed flow under `seed/flows/` must create **its own** users, batch, sections, and lectures.
- Namespace human-readable identifiers by flow id (for example `{flowId}.student@example.com`, `SDE Batch 42 [{flowId}]`).
- Flows must not assume exclusive ownership of shared rows; all flows must work when seeded back-to-back with `--no-reset`.
- Reuse factories from `seed/factories/` and shared builders — never duplicate raw insert logic inside flow seeders.
